vulkan-game
  server.cpp                server application, authoritative world and rules
  client.cpp                client application and input settings
  srv/                      game simulation, scene serialization and authority
  clt/                      client rendering, prediction and weapon presentation
  shd/                      game shaders
  assets/                   game art

Build:
  c build vulkan-server
  c build vulkan-client
The build uses VulkanEngine plus direct standalone VulkanGPU, VulkanRender,
VulkanBVH, VulkanSpatial, VulkanJobs and VulkanNetwork modules from
vulkan-systems; it no longer uses the removed root build.c. VulkanNetwork uses Mbed TLS
3.6.7 with DTLS 1.2 and forward-secret ECDHE-AES-GCM cipher suites.
The single supported transport uses a persistent server identity and
per-destination trust-on-first-use (TOFU) public-key pinning.
All gameplay, movement, weapon and reliable scene packets use the DTLS
socket; there is no plaintext application fallback.
The game owns its scene and authority protocols; network/session is reusable.

Networking (only one encrypted path):
  c build run vulkan-server
  c build run vulkan-client --host 127.0.0.1 --port 27015
  c build run vulkan-client --host SERVER_PUBLIC_IP --port 27015

  The server listens on UDP 0.0.0.0:27015 by default, accepts both LAN and
  remote clients, and generates/loads its server private key automatically.
  Forward UDP 27015 from the router to the server and allow it in the
  server firewall. If running on a VPS, open its cloud firewall for UDP.
  The client always uses DTLS (no raw-UDP gameplay or plaintext fallback).
  The game uses the same code path for local, LAN, and Internet sessions.

  On first contact the client automatically records the server's public
  key fingerprint for the configured host:port. Later connections require
  that same key. A changed key fails closed, and the client must not silently
  overwrite its stored pin. Automatic reconnect re-handshakes using the
  established identity, with no user intervention after the initial IP entry.

  Trust-on-first-use (TOFU) cannot independently establish the authenticity
  of a first-ever Internet connection against an active on-path attacker.
  The server logs a fingerprint which users can compare through a trusted
  channel when stronger first-contact authentication is required. Automatic
  TOFU authenticates servers after pinning, NOT individual game accounts.
  Use an application-level admission and permissions layer for private servers.
  Back up the server identity before moving/reinstalling the server machine.

  Credentials/pins live under the current user's OS config directory,
  not in the game repo. No CA, certificate file, key environment variables,
  pre-shared client keys, OpenSSL commands or alternative transport modes.
  IPv6 works when the server is bound to a compatible IPv6 address.

  The client must know the server IP or DNS name and UDP port. Server
  discovery, automatic router port forwarding, NAT traversal and relays
  are not performed. When the server is behind CGNAT, ordinary inbound
  port forwarding cannot expose it without a public endpoint or relay.

Security and deployment limits:
  TOFU pins a server public key; it does not authenticate clients or players.
  Production deployments need admission controls, permissions, rate limits,
  flood protection, incident response and packet-loss testing.
  The transport is DTLS 1.2, not QUIC/DTLS 1.3. DTLS protects packet
  confidentiality, integrity and replay; reliable bulk scene transfer is
  handled separately by network/session. No bandwidth, latency or GPU
  frame-time guarantee is implied.

Native compilation, handshake interoperability and platform/runtime regression
testing are required before treating this revision as a release.

Platform requirements:
  macOS: Vulkan via MoltenVK; Linux: Vulkan driver and X11/XWayland;
  Windows: Vulkan driver and GLFW Win32. The shader compiler
  glslangValidator must be on PATH. VulkanEngine's generated API needs
  Python 3 (python3 on Unix, python on Windows). Platform builds and
  runtime compatibility still require verification on each OS.

Rendering status:
  Rendering uses Rogue/hellengine-bak's world-space point-cloud and 3D probe
  approach: static collision surfaces provide 0.3-unit lighting samples, and
  RGBA16F 32x22x36 probes at 0.2-unit spacing provide diffuse GI.
  Point-light shadows and probe visibility now trace the actual streamed
  mesh triangles (including imported models) through a compact stackless BVH
  built when a scene snapshot changes. This replaces the old collision-box
  shadow silhouettes and brute-force per-ray triangle scans.
  Skinned meshes are sampled at their initial pose for shadow construction;
  continuous animation-driven BVH updates are not implemented.
  Alpha-blended surfaces do not cast opaque shadows; masked materials still
  use triangle silhouettes, not texture-based alpha tests.
  Space visualizes every third probe as a sphere without changing GI.
  Vulkan shaders, static light positions and level configuration remain
  game-specific; reusable Vulkan modules stay generic.

  The finite probe volume is centered around the demo scene. This is not a
  literal OpenGL cubemap-PCF port, and performance, native compilation, and
  Vulkan validation on macOS/Linux/Windows remain unverified.

Rendering and reusable systems:
  VulkanRender owns general indexed geometry buffers, sampled G-buffer
  attachment images, render target/frame boilerplate and ordered GPU graph
  recording. The client supplies scene snapshots, material/animation payloads,
  GPU draw parameters, game-specific deferred shaders and presentation policy.
  clt/render/passes.* is removed: clt/render/renderer.cpp submits the six
  ordered geometry, cloud, probe, light, tone and presentation passes through
  render::sequence. GI policy remains in the game shaders,
  not in the shared VulkanGPU/Render libraries. The existing game uses GPU culling and
  streamed indexed meshes through render::geometry with nonblocking uploads.

  Emissive RGB is packed through the otherwise unused alpha channels of the
  existing three G-buffer targets, avoiding a fourth target. The light pass
  computes per-pixel view rays using the camera FOV and basis vectors.
  BVH TLAS/BLAS ray queries remain an optional VulkanBVH capability and are
  not dispatched by the normal rasterization path; no BVH work is performed
  without a consumer requiring ray/visibility queries.
